it is really cold here today minus 8  degrees in the eastern city of kharkiv  the losses are so great that reports are  rendered in numbers  400 buildings destroyed since the war  began  including 48 schools 190 civilians dead  residential areas struck 89 times in a  single day  as if counting them somehow completes  the picture as one bombed out building  starts to look much like another  they don't wait for air raid sirens  anymore in kharkiv many haven't left the  city's metro stations for more than a  week  here is where i sleep this young woman  says  and over there are my things and there  is where we all sit  ever more civilians are getting caught  up in this war ukraine's defense  minister says russia has killed more  innocent people than it has ukrainian  soldiers one day war historians may  argue about which city had it worst  today though it is surely mario pole  that is most like ukraine's stalingrad  besieged for more than ten days hundreds  of thousands unable to escape deprived  of water and heat in temperatures well  below zero a red cross official in the  city describes children going hungry a  black market in vegetables fights  breaking out over food conditions the  mayor calls medieval  critical infrastructure continues to get  hit with a special focus on military  airfields like here in lutsk north east  of kiev and ivanov frankevsk in the west  as evacuation routes continue out of  some embattled places a tide of humanity  is shifting across this country and not  all of it heading for the border the  united nations estimates a million  people have become displaced internally  seeking shelter where they can find it  like zhenya the 24 year old mother of  three stoking the wood fire in an  abandoned farmhouse 600 kilometers from  her home outside kiev  they even brought the dog a labrador pup  called girder  but they didn't bring dad  he stayed behind to join the territorial  defense  the children know that their dad is  defending the city they understand that  i believe in one year it will be over  and will be able to go back  zhenya and her children are being  relocated now to a better home in the  village this tiny rural outpost in  western ukraine like so many others  throwing their arms wide open to fellow  ukrainians fleeing the war  the un also estimates that 12 million  people inside ukraine will need  humanitarian help  and few besides zhenya are guessing how  long it might be before they're able to  go home joe nahul al jazeera love
 

